"Automated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Lear (https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf)\nciteturn34view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view0\",\"pattern\":\"92%\"}); Total lines: 773\nNo matching text found for \"92%\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutomated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Lear (https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf)\nciteturn34view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view0\",\"pattern\":\"RESULTS\"}); Total lines: 773\nL302@P7: system. All learners answered the questions in the same order. The questionnaire was\nL303@P7: administered in German and is also available in English at http://tinyurl.com/2d8ksvah.\nL304@P7-8: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 7Rater training and rating form\nL305@P8: After recruiting the 14 raters, we first trained them during two three-hour workshops\nL306@P8: as part of their teacher training at the university. For each performance, the raters\nL307@P8: scored the five different aspects according to the same scheme as used in the Demo:\nL308@P8: handshape individually for both hands (as either correct or incorrect), hand movement\nL309@P8: individually for both hands (on a scale from 1 to 4), and overall score (on a scale from\nL310@P8: 0 to 4, see Table 1 above). In the first workshop, we introduced the rating procedure\nL311@P8: and rating scale and asked them to familiarise themselves with it. Following this\nL312@P8: introduction, the raters rated an example performance on all five aspects during the\nL313@P8: workshop, and the results were compared and discussed. For the overall score, the\nL314@P8: raters were trained to base the ratings on their overall impression of the handshape\nL315@P8: and hand movement of both hands, as conveyed in the rating scale descriptors. After\nL316@P8: the initial training workshop, the raters received an Excel rating form including video\nL317@P8: links to three more example performances and they were asked to rate these on all\nL318@P8: aspects individually at home. During the next workshop one week later, the ratings\nL319@P8: were again compared and discussed as a group, to ensure that there was a mutual\nL320@P8: understanding of the scale descriptors and the procedure. In the course of the work\u0002shops, we also refined the rating instructions and changed the Excel rating form\nL321@P8: slightly to make them clearer.\nL322@P8: After training, we sent the individual Excel rating forms including detailed\nL323@P8: instructions to all raters and asked them to complete the forms within one\nL324@P8: month. The final version of the rating forms included the gloss (meaning of the\nL325@P8: lexeme) and video link for each performance, as well as individual columns for the\nL326@P8: five aspects. The raters scored each aspect by marking the relevant cells with an X.\nL327@P8: The scale descriptors and rating instructions were also included in the rating form.\nL328@P8: An example rating form (translated from German to English) is included in\nL329@P8: Appendix 2.\nL330@P8: Rating design\nL331@P8: The 768 performances (16 learners × 16 lexemes × 3 attempts) were scored by the 14\nL332@P8: raters on the five different aspects in an overlapping design, whereby all raters scored\nL333@P8: performances by all learners, on all lexemes, and for each of the three attempts. We\nL334@P8: ran a Python script to produce the 14 individual rating forms to ensure that the\nL335@P8: performances were allocated equally across raters in terms of the three different\nL336@P8: variables (learners, lexemes, and attempts). The script also controlled for the order of\nL337@P8: performances (e.g., if a particular learner, lexeme, or attempt would consistently have\nL338@P8: been rated last by many of the raters, this might have impacted the validity of the\nL339@P8: findings; controlling for the order was therefore important). In addition, the design\nL340@P8: included 96 anchor performances which were scored by all raters. The anchor\nL341@P8: performances were distributed equally across the three variables, and the position\nL342@P8: of the anchor videos within each rating form was also controlled for in the Python\nL343@P8: script to avoid potential ordering effects. Due to this research design, each rater\nL344@P8: scored between 143 and 145 performances (47 to 49 unique performances + 96\nL345@P8: anchor performances).\nL360@P9: Rater groupings were used to compare the severity of the automated rater to the human\nL361@P9: raters using Wald t statistics (see Eckes, 2015). Facets bias/interaction analyses were\nL362@P9: employed to investigate differences regarding Criterion, Lexeme, and Attempt.\nL363@P9: The questionnaire data for the closed questions were analysed descriptively by calculat\u0002ing the median answer for each statement. Open-ended responses were first grouped\nL364@P9: according to the six open-ended questions:\nL365@P9: (1) What was not clear in the instructions? (5 responses)\nL366@P9: (2) Which technical problems occurred? (4 responses)\nL367@P9: (3) Did you experience any particular difficulties when completing the exercise? If yes,\nL368@P9: please briefly explain which difficulties. (6 responses)\nL369@P9: (4) Do you have any suggestions on how the feedback could be improved to better\nL370@P9: support you in learning DSGS? (7 responses)\nL371@P9: (5) Do you have any suggestions for other formats to help you learn DSGS even better?\nL372@P9: (2 responses)\nL373@P9: (6) Is there anything else you would like to tell us about this exercise (e.g. suggestions on\nL374@P9: how the exercise could be improved)? (3 responses)\nL375@P9: In a second step, the first author coded the responses for common themes, following the six\nL376@P9: phases of thematic analysis suggested by Brown and Clarke (2006). Through this process, 24\nL377@P9: out of 27 responses could be unambiguously assigned one theme, while the remaining three\nL378@P9: responses were assigned two themes. The five identified themes were:\nL379@P9: (1) Unclear instructions (5 responses)\nL380@P9: (2) Technical problems (4 responses)\nL381@P9: (3) Incorrect Demo feedback (12 responses)\nL382@P9: (4) Suggested test formats (4 responses)\nL383@P9: (5) Positive learner feedback (5 responses)\nL384@P9-10: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 9RESULTS\nL385@P10: MFRM measures and model fit\nL386@P10: Before presenting results on the research questions, this section outlines MFRM measures to\nL387@P10: establish the extent to which our dataset cooperated with Rasch model requirements. Overall\nL388@P10: model fit was good, with Rasch measures accounting for 71.37% of the variance and the global\nL389@P10: chi square with a probability of .58. Rater fit indices were very close to the expected value of 1,\nL390@P10: with small standard deviations, indicating uniformity of fit. Rater Infit values ranged from .83\nL391@P10: through 1.29, and Outfit values ranged from .72 through 1.16, indicating no serious depar\u0002tures from fit to the Rasch model (Wright & Linacre, 1994). A Rasch κ coefficient of 0.22 on\nL392@P10: the rater facet indicates a moderate level of rater agreement. Raters could be separated into\nL393@P10: seven distinct levels of severity. As expected, learners also had a range of abilities, being\nL394@P10: divisible into four distinct levels. The learners' overall ability was high compared to the\nL395@P10: difficulty of the lexemes, which was expected as most of the signs in the Demo were familiar\nL396@P10: to them, so their first attempt was rated highly. In addition, the second attempt was rated\nL397@P10: highly as learners were encouraged to produce the signs as best as they could modelling the\nL398@P10: reference video. Lexemes could be separated into six levels. The three attempts had a very wide\nL399@P10: range of difficulty, as would be hoped, given the inclusion of a “deliberate mistake” attempt.\nL400@P10: The Criterion facet also had a wide range of difficulty, ranging from the non-dominant\nL401@P10: handshape with a logit of − 0.64 through the overall criterion with a logit value of 0.58,\nL402@P10: representing 13 distinct levels of difficulty. All reliability of separation coefficients were 0.95\nL403@P10: or higher. See Table 2 and Figure 1 for a summary of measures and model fit.\nL404@P10: Table 2. MFRM Summary Statistics.\nL405@P10: Raters\nL406@P10: All Human Automated Learner Lexeme Attempt Criterion\nL407@P10: N 15 14 1 16 16 3 5\nL408@P10: Measures\nL409@P10: Mean 0.00 −0.02 0.25 1.31 0.00 0.00 0.00\nL410@P10: SD (sample) 0.38 0.39 0.00 0.21 0.31 0.37 0.49\nL411@P10: SE 0.05 0.05 0.02 0.05 0.05 0.02 0.03\nL412@P10: RMSE (sample) 0.05 0.05 0.05 0.02 0.04\nL531@P14: This study investigated the extent to which human expert raters agree with automated\nL532@P14: ratings of the Web SMILE Demo, an application designed for learning and automatically\nL533@P14: assessing the production of individual lexemes in sign languages. We also studied learners’\nL534@P14: perception of the automated scores and the automated feedback generated through the\nL535@P14: Demo by means of a questionnaire. The study was conducted in the context of DSGS.\nL536@P14: The results indicate that the Web SMILE Demo was more severe than an average human\nL537@P14: rater, however it showed a high level of agreement with the human raters nearest to it in\nL538@P14: severity. In terms of the different rating criteria, the automated ratings on the non-dominant\nL539@P14: hand showed larger discrepancies with the human ratings than the ratings on the dominant\nL540@P14: hand (58% of comparisons with significant differences were observed for the non-dominant\nL541@P14: hand, compared to 25% for the dominant hand). Subsequent analyses indicated that the\nL542@P14: automated rater picked up on small movements of the non-dominant hand even for one\u0002handed signs and rated them lower, whereas human raters would likely have considered small\nL543@P14: movements as acceptable. This finding is in line with comparisons of human and automated\nL544@P14: ratings for assessing writing or speaking performances in spoken languages, where high levels\nL545@P14: of overall agreement between humans and machines are usually reported, despite some\nL546@P14: deviations in severity for certain aspects (for assessing writing see e.g. Chan et al., 2023;\nL547@P14: Correnti et al., 2020; Hussein et al., 2019; for assessing speaking see e.g.; Xi et al., 2012; Xu et\nL548@P14: al., 2021). Thus, the automated ratings reported through the Web SMILE Demo can be said to\nL549@P14: reflect human expert ratings well, although they were slightly more severe than an average\nL550@P14: human rater, particularly for rating handshape and hand movement of the non-dominant\nL551@P14: hand. Apart from displaying slightly-above average rater severity, the many-facet Rasch\nL552@P14: model also showed that the Web SMILE Demo rated consistently, with an InfitMS value of\nL553@P14: 1.10. This indicates a good level of intra-rater reliability.\nL554@P14: Despite good agreement between the automated system with (slightly severe) human\nL555@P14: expert raters, as well as high intra-rater reliability, learner feedback questionnaire data\nL653@P17: Eckes, T. (2015). Introduction to many-facet Rasch measurement: Analyzing and evaluating rater\u0002mediated assessments (2nd ed.). Peter Lang. https://doi.org/10.3726/978-3-653-04844-5\nL654@P17: Ellis, K., Fisher, J., Willoughby, L., & Barca, J. C. (2016). A design science exploration of a visual-spatial\nL655@P17: learning system with feedback. Paper presented at the Australasian Conference on Information\nL656@P17: Systems 2015, Adelaide. https://doi.org/10.48550/ARXIV.1606.03509\nL657@P17: Fenlon, J., & Hochgesang, J. A. (Eds.). (2022). Signed language corpora. Gallaudet University Press.\nL658@P17: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctv2rcnfhc\nL659@P17: Hanke, T., & Fenlon, J. (2022). Creating corpora: Data collection. In J. Fenlon & J. A. Hochgesang\nL660@P17: (Eds.), Signed language corpora (pp. 18–45). Gallaudet University Press. https://doi.org/10.2307/j.\nL661@P17: ctv2rcnfhc.7\nL662@P17: Haug, T., & Holzknecht, F. (2020). Using automatic sign language recognition for sentence-level\nL663@P17: assessment of Swiss German Sign Language – SMILE-II. Paper presented at the 2020 Language\nL664@P17: Testing Research Colloquium, online.\nL665@P17: Haug, T., Mann, W., & Holzknecht, F. (2023). The use of technology in sign language testing: Results\nL666@P17: of a pre-pandemic survey. Sign Language Studies, 23(2), 243–281. https://doi.org/10.1353/sls.2023.\nL667@P17: 0003\nL668@P17: Humphries, T., Kushalnagar, P., Mathur, G., Napoli, D. J., Padden, C., Rathmann, C., & Smith, S.\nL669@P17: (2016). Language choices for deaf infants: Advice for parents regarding sign languages. Clinical\nL670@P17: Pediatrics, 55(6), 513–517. https://doi.org/10.1177/0009922815616891\nL671@P17: Hussein, M. A., Hassan, H., & Nassef, M. (2019). Automated language essay scoring systems:\nL672@P17: Aliterature review. Peer J Computer Science, 5, e208. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.208\nL673@P17: Jin, Y. (2023). Test-taker insights for language assessment policies and practices. Language Testing, 40\nL674@P17: (1), 193–203. https://doi.org/10.1177/02655322221117136\nL675@P17: Linacre, J. M. (1994). Many-facet Rasch measurement (2nd ed.). MESA Press.\nL676@P17: Linacre, J. M. (2023). Facets (3.85.1). Winsteps.com. http://www.winsteps.com/facets.htm\nL677@P17: Liu, Z., Pang, L., & Qi, X. (2024). MEN: Mutual enhancement networks for sign language recognition\nL678@P17: and education. IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems, 35(1), 311–325.\nL679@P17: https://doi.org/10.1109/TNNLS.2022.3174031\nL680@P17: Marian, V., Blumenfeld, H. K., & Kaushanskaya, M. (2007). The language experience and proficiency\nL681@P17: questionnaire (LEAP-Q): Assessing language profiles in bilinguals and multilinguals. Journal of\nL682@P17: Speech, Language, and Hearing Research, 50(4), 940–967. https://doi.org/10.1044/1092-4388(2007/\nL683@P17: 067)\nL684@P17: Phan, H. D., Ellis, K., & Dorin, A. (2018). MIC, an interactive sign language teaching system. In D.\nL685@P17: McKay, J. Waycott, A. Morrison, J. -H. -J. Choi, A. Lugmayr, M. Billinghurst, R. Kelly, G. Buchanan,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutomated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Lear (https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf)\nciteturn34view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view0\",\"pattern\":\"correlation\"}); Total lines: 773\nNo matching text found for \"correlation\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutomated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Lear (https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf)\nciteturn34view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view0\",\"pattern\":\"incorrect\"}); Total lines: 773\nL74@P2: systems have been developed over the last years. For example, Ellis et al. (2016) and Phan et\nL75@P2: al. (2018) developed a feedback system for Auslan (Australian Sign Language) that rates\nL76@P2: individual signs recorded through a Microsoft Kinect camera as either correct or incorrect\nL77@P2: by comparing them to a reference video. The system also provides visual feedback on\nL78@P2: incorrectly performed signs using avatars. Another system that requires users to be\nL169@P4: After processing, the Demo provides automated feedback on five different aspects of the\nL170@P4: learner’s performance: handshape is assessed as either correct or incorrect for both domi\u0002nant and non-dominant hand (signers usually have a dominant hand which they use to\nL171@P4: produce one-handed signs, see Watkins & Thompson, 2017), hand movement is rated on a\nL172@P4: scale for both hands, and overall score is rated according to the same scale as hand\nL173@P4: Table 1. Rating Scale for Hand Movement and Overall\nL174@P4: Score.\nL190@P5: In addition to the scores, feedback is provided visually using the reference-video’s\nL191@P5: skeleton overlaid with the learner-video’s skeleton, whereby incorrect handshapes are\nL192@P5: marked by a red circle and hand movements by a red line in the feedback video. This\nL193@P5: enables learners to see at which point during the production and for which aspect an error\nL194@P5: occurred and for how long it lasted. It can be argued that this type of feedback is in line with\nL277@P7: each lexeme three times: the first time without looking at the reference video, the second\nL278@P7: time with looking at the reference video and to the best of their ability, and the third time\nL279@P7: with a deliberate mistake in their production such as incorrect handshape or hand move\u0002ment. This enabled us to systematically investigate the extent to which the Web SMILE\nL280@P7: Demo detected errors as compared to the human raters. Feedback was provided to the\nL281@P7: learners after each recording.\nL308@P8: handshape individually for both hands (as either correct or incorrect), hand movement\nL309@P8: individually for both hands (on a scale from 1 to 4), and overall score (on a scale from\nL310@P8: 0 to 4, see Table 1 above). In the first workshop, we introduced the rating procedure\nL311@P8: and rating scale and asked them to familiarise themselves with it. Following this\nL312@P8: introduction, the raters rated an example performance on all five aspects during the\nL379@P9: (1) Unclear instructions (5 responses)\nL380@P9: (2) Technical problems (4 responses)\nL381@P9: (3) Incorrect Demo feedback (12 responses)\nL382@P9: (4) Suggested test formats (4 responses)\nL383@P9: (5) Positive learner feedback (5 responses)\nL384@P9-10: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 9RESULTS\nL385@P10: MFRM measures and model fit\nL386@P10: Before presenting results on the research questions, this section outlines MFRM measures to\nL387@P10: establish the extent to which our dataset cooperated with Rasch model requirements. Overall\nL505@P13: felt that they could not showcase their productive DSGS-competence very well,\nL506@P13: although the group as a whole agreed to this statement (Mdn = 5). One reason for\nL507@P13: this may be that many learners may have already known many of the lexemes and may\nL508@P13: therefore not have been able to show their full productive DSGS-proficiency. Some of\nL509@P13: the learners also suggested other test format for measuring productive signing in their\nL510@P13: open responses (Theme 4), for example “sentence-level assessments”.\nL511@P13: The statement “The feedback helps me to improve my DSGS-competence” received\nL512@P13: the lowest level of agreement, with six learners partly disagreeing and one learner fully\nL513@P13: disagreeing (Mdn = 4 “partly agree”). The reasons why this statement received a\nL514@P13: relatively low level of agreement become clear when looking at the learners’ open\nL515@P13: answers: Incorrect Demo feedback (Theme 3) was the most common theme across all\nL516@P13: responses, with 6 out of 16 students mentioning in 12 responses that they sometimes\nL517@P13: did not understand why the Demo rated certain aspects as incorrect. Exemplary\nL518@P13: comments were:\nL519@P13: Learner 1: Sometimes I couldn’t make out why the program declared something as correct or\nL520@P13: incorrect. For example, with [“von”] I thought it must be because of small discrepancies in the\nL521@P13: recording angle.\nL522@P13: Learner 5: [. . .] I was irritated that I sometimes received the message “The target sign was\nL523@P13: incorrect” when I definitely did not see an error.\nL524@P13: Figure 2. Student Questionnaire Responses. *The two statements on difficulty were reverse-coded.\nL525@P13-14: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 13Learner 12: Sometimes it was not clear for me at all which mistakes I had made or where the\nL526@P14: differences between my video and the reference video were, so I could not make any\nL527@P14: improvements.\nL528@P14: Learner 16: In isolated cases it was not clear for me what the mistake was. For example, when I\nL529@P14: put down my hand [at the end of a sign], this was also rated.\nL530@P14: DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION\nL531@P14: This study investigated the extent to which human expert raters agree with automated\nL532@P14: ratings of the Web SMILE Demo, an application designed for learning and automatically\nL533@P14: assessing the production of individual lexemes in sign languages. We also studied learners’\nL534@P14: perception of the automated scores and the automated feedback generated through the\nL535@P14: Demo by means of a questionnaire. The study was conducted in the context of DSGS.\nL536@P14: The results indicate that the Web SMILE Demo was more severe than an average human\nL537@P14: rater, however it showed a high level of agreement with the human raters nearest to it in\nL538@P14: severity. In terms of the different rating criteria, the automated ratings on the non-dominant\nL539@P14: hand showed larger discrepancies with the human ratings than the ratings on the dominant\nL540@P14: hand (58% of comparisons with significant differences were observed for the non-dominant\nL541@P14: hand, compared to 25% for the dominant hand). Subsequent analyses indicated that the\nL542@P14: automated rater picked up on small movements of the non-dominant hand even for one\u0002handed signs and rated them lower, whereas human raters would likely have considered small\nL543@P14: movements as acceptable. This finding is in line with comparisons of human and automated\nL544@P14: ratings for assessing writing or speaking performances in spoken languages, where high levels\nL545@P14: of overall agreement between humans and machines are usually reported, despite some\nL546@P14: deviations in severity for certain aspects (for assessing writing see e.g. Chan et al., 2023;\nL547@P14: Correnti et al., 2020; Hussein et al., 2019; for assessing speaking see e.g.; Xi et al., 2012; Xu et\nL548@P14: al., 2021). Thus, the automated ratings reported through the Web SMILE Demo can be said to\nL549@P14: reflect human expert ratings well, although they were slightly more severe than an average\nL579@P15: for specific aspects of a performance that do not reflect the actual performance. A dis\u0002advantage of automated systems is that they usually do not outline to the user how they\nL580@P15: reached a certain score. Although Machine Learning models are transparent and can be\nL581@P15: explained, the underlying algorithms are often not comprehendible for lay-people. One\nL582@P15: option to address this shortcoming may be to give learners the opportunity to ask for\nL583@P15: (human) clarification in case the automated feedback is obviously incorrect. Once the\nL584@P15: system has been comprehensively validated to ensure that incorrect ratings do not occur,\nL585@P15: human clarifications may no longer be necessary.\nL586@P15: Apart from some of the automated ratings impacting the overall quality of feedback,\nL587@P15: learners generally enjoyed working with the Web SMILE Demo. They thought that the\nL588@P15: automated system was interesting and fun, and that it measured competencies which the\nL589@P15: learners needed for sign language communication. This indicates that the tasks were authen\u0002tic and elicit competencies that mirror those needed in real-life settings, which is another\nL590@P15: necessary prerequisite for successful LOA (Carless et al., 2006). In addition, for many of the\nL591@P15: signs and in many instances, the Demo provided useful feedback on the correctness of\nL592@P15: handshape and hand movement for both dominant and non-dominant hand. The Demo was\nL593@P15: successful in helping learners practice individual signs in DSGS, and to perform the manual\nL594@P15: aspects of these signs accurately. The fact that the learners provided insightful comments on\nL595@P15: the feedback they received also shows that they developed and applied their evaluative\nL596@P15: expertise, which addresses the third key principle of LOA (Carless, 2015). Their comments\nL597@P15: showed that improvements need to be made on rating accuracy, for example regarding the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutomated Sign Language Vocabulary Assessment: Comparing Human and Machine Ratings and Studying Lear (https://publications.idiap.ch/attachments/papers/2024/Holzknecht_LAQ_2024.pdf)\nciteturn34view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32view0\",\"pattern\":\"learning outcomes\"}); Total lines: 773\nL105@P3: statistically analyze their agreement and the extent to which they measure the same under\u0002lying trait (Van Moere & Downey, 2016). Although limited studies involving only one\nL106@P3: human rater have been conducted for some of the systems presented above (Chai et al.,\nL107@P3: 2017; Liu et al., 2024), comprehensive studies are still lacking.\nL108@P3: In addition, as the main objective of all existing automated sign language assessments is\nL109@P3: to promote and foster learning, they should also be studied from a learning-oriented\nL110@P3: assessment (LOA) perspective. LOA has traditionally been under-represented in the assess\u0002ment literature, and only in the last decade have researchers begun to more systematically\nL111@P3: focus on the perspectives and insights of the learners themselves (Jin, 2023). Carless and\nL112@P3: colleagues propose three key components that are central to LOA approaches:\nL113@P3: 1. Designing LOA tasks. The tasks learners need to complete should be authentic and elicit\nL114@P3: competencies that mirror those needed in real-life.\nL115@P3: 2. Developing learners’ evaluative expertise. Learners should develop skills that enable them to\nL116@P3: critically analyze their work and the work of their peers with a view to support learning.\nL117@P3: 3. Learners’ engagement with feedback. Learners need to understand and actively engage with\nL118@P3: the feedback provided to them so they can use it appropriately to improve learning\nL119@P3: outcomes.\nL120@P3: (Carless, 2015; Carless et al., 2006, pp.964–965).\nL121@P3: Automated sign language assessment and feedback systems should ideally also encapsulate\nL122@P3: these three components. However, it has not been studied whether and to what extent these\nL123@P3: principles are met in existing systems.\nL124@P3: The current paper addresses some of these challenges. Our aim was to validate an\nL125@P3: automated sign language assessment and feedback system for Swiss German Sign\nL126@P3: Language (Deutschschweizerische Gebärdensprache, DSGS), which is based on a large\nL127@P3: dataset of signed L1 and L2 productions, through (1) comparisons of automated ratings\nL128@P3: with those of human expert raters, and (2) learner feedback questionnaire data on the\nL129@P3: usefulness of the system with regards to improved learning outcomes. We first briefly\nL130@P3: describe the system in the next section before outlining the study in detail.\nL131@P3: THE WEB SMILE DEMO\nL132@P3: The Web SMILE Demo is an application designed for learning and automatically assessing\nL133@P3: the production of individual lexemes in sign languages. The current version of the Demo\nL134@P3: was developed in the course of the SMILE (Scalable Multimodal Sign Language Technology\nL135@P3: for Sign Language Learning and Assessment) research projects on DSGS (Ebling et al., 2018;\nL136@P3: Haug & Holzknecht, 2020). Like the systems outlined above, the Web SMILE Demo\nL137@P3: currently only provides scores and feedback on manual components of individual lexemes\nL138@P3: and does not (yet) consider non-manuals. However, in contrast to other existing systems, it\nL139@P3-4: LANGUAGE ASSESSMENT QUARTERLY 3is based on a dataset of performances consisting of multiple productions of 100 DSGS\nL140@P4: lexemes by 9 L1 DSGS signers and 19 L2 DSGS learners, which have been annotated using\nL141@P4: six category/acceptability criteria at lexeme-level: “(1) same lexeme as target sign (same\nL142@P4: meaning, same form), (2) same lexeme as target sign (same meaning, slightly different\nL143@P4: form), (3) same lexeme as target sign (same meaning, different form), (4) same lexeme as\nL144@P4: target sign (slightly different meaning, slightly different form), (5) different lexeme than\nL145@P4: target sign (same meaning, different form), (6) different lexeme than target sign (different\nL146@P4: meaning, different form)” (see Ebling et al., 2018). In addition, the Demo does not require\nL147@P4: special external camera equipment or specific server capacities but can be run online on any\nL148@P4: modern computer with an integrated or connected webcam. The Demo currently includes\nL149@P4: 16 DSGS lexemes, but the number of lexemes will be extended to 100 during the next\nL150@P4: project (for details about the 16 lexemes, including pictures and links to videos, please see\nL151@P4: Appendix 1). Although it is only available for DSGS, the Demo’s underlying algorithms can\nL152@P4: be run on data from any sign language. We used the first version of the Demo for the\nL153@P4: current study (see Tornay et al., 2020).\nL154@P4: To practice or be assessed on the production of lexemes with the Web SMILE Demo,\nL155@P4: learners need a personal computer with a webcam and internet access. After logging in via a\nL156@P4: web browser, learners first need to sign a data protection consent form. They are then\nL157@P4: directed to a“How it works” section, which describes the different processes and subpages\nL158@P4: and includes contact details in case of technical or other problems.\n"
